A controlled component receives its current `value` and calls `onChange`; an uncontrolled one uses internal state initialized from `defaultValue`. Pick the mode from whether `value` is present, and do not switch modes during the component's lifetime. Normalize an absent controlled text value to `''` and a checkbox value to a boolean so the DOM does not see `undefined` first and a real value later. In controlled mode, update the backing state synchronously from the event. Do not maintain a second competing value. For a reusable component, warn in development if the caller changes modes, and test initial value, edits, reset, and both modes.

```ts caption="Keep one source of truth while allowing either ownership mode."
const isControlled = value !== undefined;
const current = isControlled ? value : internalValue;

function handleChange(next: string) {
  if (!isControlled) setInternalValue(next);
  onChange?.(next);
}
```

Model query, suggestions, active option, and request status separately; derive visible options rather than duplicating them in state. Debounce the side effect, not the displayed input, and cancel the timer during cleanup. Give each request an `AbortController` or equivalent ownership mechanism so an older response cannot replace newer results; still ignore abort errors and surface real failures. Define a minimum query length and show loading, empty, and error states. On selection, update the committed value, close the popup, and preserve the input's focus behavior. Combine this with the WAI-ARIA combobox contract instead of treating accessibility as decorative attributes.

```ts caption="Cancel both scheduled work and the in-flight request for the previous query."
useEffect(() => {
  const controller = new AbortController();
  const timer = window.setTimeout(() => {
    search(query, { signal: controller.signal });
  }, 250);
  return () => {
    window.clearTimeout(timer);
    controller.abort();
  };
}, [query, search]);
```
